# The wolf-lang commit this repo is keyed to. A bump is its own commit, with
# the re-vendored data, and nothing else (vendor/README.md).
#
# le04 pins the RELEASE TAG rather than a trunk commit: `v0.2.1` points at
# this sha. Trunk has moved past it (r04 merged after the release), and the
# next bump may well be a trunk sha again — nothing here requires a tag.
commit = 75fd2d0b25b6ed995ec7293053bc32fb3497e272
# The exact `wolf --version` string that commit produces (first line; the
# binary now prints a second line naming its lupin pairing). `lspconf doctor`
# fails on a mismatch: a stale local `wolf` producing green transcripts is the
# failure mode the pin exists to prevent.
#
# D57 decides the shape, and the shape CHANGED at this bump. A build is a
# release build only when the builder stamps BOTH `WOLF_COMMIT=<short sha>`
# and `WOLF_RELEASE=v{crate version}`, and the second is granted only when
# that exact tag points at HEAD. Every previous pin was an off-tag commit, so
# every previous pinned string carried `+dev.<sha>`; this one is at v0.2.1, so
# the release build prints the BARE version and the pin clause. An unstamped
# build still prints `+dev.unknown` — the string any trunk build of the same
# crate version prints, which is exactly the stale-binary hole — so the pinned
# string remains a stamped build's, and `cargo xtask dist` in the submodule is
# the builder that computes the stamp (README §Running the server lane
# locally; its plain-cargo recipe cannot grant the release half).
#
# `xtask compat`'s `version_number` reads the number out of this string; it
# learned semver build metadata at le03 and takes the bare tag form
# unchanged — `0.2.1`, no `+` to strip. Asserted in xtask/src/compat.rs.
#
# The pin clause is EIGHT hex digits here, and that was measured, not chosen.
# wolf-lang's `git_short_sha()` runs `git rev-parse --short HEAD` with git's
# AUTO abbreviation, which has just grown from seven to eight in a full clone
# of that repo. The README's old local recipe hard-coded `--short=7` and so
# would print `pin 75fd2d0` — a string `doctor` refuses against this pin. The
# recipe now mirrors upstream's own command instead of restating it. That the
# identity string's width depends on how big the reader's clone is, is an
# upstream finding, not a thing to paper over here.
version = "wolf 0.2.1 (wolfgang, pin 75fd2d0b)"
# Does `wolf lsp` exist at this commit? YES — wolf-lang s52 landed it
# (`wolf: the lsp subcommand`, 07e553d). This flip is what lights every
# server-dependent lane: record, replay, onetruth, bench, and the encoding,
# cancellation, and fuzz suites all reach `Availability::Ready` as soon as a
# binary at this version resolves.
#
# The acquire step is dark for a NEW reason at this pin, and the change is
# worth reading. Every earlier pin was an off-tag sha, so no published asset
# could ever match it. This pin IS a release tag, and wolf-lang's v0.2.1
# release does carry four tier-1 assets — but the release is a **DRAFT**
# (`gh release list --repo wolffe-lang/wolf-lang`, 2026-09-01: v0.2.1 Draft,
# v0.2.0 Draft, v0.1.0 Latest). A draft's assets sit behind an `untagged-…`
# URL and need an authenticated request, so an unauthenticated acquire still
# gets nothing. The gate moved from "no asset exists at this sha" to "the
# asset exists and is unpublished" — reported upstream, not worked around.
serves_lsp = true
